Accommodation Research

 I researched potential accommodation in Maribor, Slovenia; Kavala, Greece; and Geel, Belgium. I focused mainly on student accommodation and reasonably priced rooms because the traineeship could last between three and six months.

------- 1. Maribor, Slovenia – PRIOT Digital Systems

Maribor is a student city, so there are several student accommodation options. The University of Maribor states that Erasmus students can apply for accommodation in its student dormitories after receiving an acceptance letter. Available locations include Gosposvetska, Tyrševa and Quadro.

Option 1 – University of Maribor Student Dormitories

Univerza v Mariboru, Študentski domovi - Dom 1

Student dormitory

(02) 228 42 16

The University of Maribor student dormitories are probably the best budget option for an Erasmus student. 
The university states that dormitory accommodation for foreign students is approximately €140–€170 per month for shared accommodation. Private rooms in shared student flats are approximately €150–€220 per month including costs.

Advantages:

Very affordable
Designed for students
Suitable for Erasmus students
Internet available
Furnished with basic bedding
Several locations around Maribor

Disadvantages:

Rooms and bathrooms/kitchens may be shared
Erasmus places are limited
Application can only be made after acceptance

Option 2 – Private Shared Student Accommodation

The University of Maribor recommends private rooms in shared student flats as another option.
The university gives an approximate price of €150–€220 per month including costs, depending on the type of room.

This could provide more privacy and flexibility than a dormitory while remaining relatively affordable.

Estimated budget: €150–€220/month.

Best choice for Maribor

University student accommodation would be my first choice because it is inexpensive and specifically supports international students.
A private shared flat would be my backup if a dormitory place was unavailable.

-----🇬🇷 2. Kavala, Greece – JP IT Solutions & Consulting

Kavala has a large student population because of the International Hellenic University, meaning student rooms and private rentals are potential options. For a three-month internship, I would particularly look for furnished rooms or short-term shared accommodation, rather than signing a long residential contract.

Option 1 – Student Room / Shared Apartment

A private room in a shared apartment would probably be one of the most practical options for a short Erasmus traineeship.

Advantages:

Usually cheaper than renting a whole apartment
Opportunity to live with other students
Kitchen and other facilities can be shared
Easier to manage for a three-month placement

Disadvantages:

Less privacy
Availability can change quickly
Bills may be separate from rent
The distance from JP IT Solutions would need to be checked before signing
Option 2 – Private Studio Apartment

A small furnished studio would provide more independence than shared accommodation.

Advantages:

Private bedroom, kitchen and bathroom
More privacy
No need to share facilities
Suitable for someone working full-time

Disadvantages:

More expensive than a shared room
Electricity, heating, water and internet may be additional costs
Short-term contracts may be more difficult to find
Option 3 – Short-Term Furnished Apartment

A furnished apartment or short-term rental could be useful initially, particularly if I needed somewhere to stay while looking for longer-term accommodation.

Advantages:

Furnished
Easier for an international student arriving from Ireland
No need to purchase furniture
Useful for a short three-month placement

Disadvantages:

Usually more expensive per month
Availability and prices can vary
Short-term rental conditions need to be checked carefully
Best choice for Kavala

For the three-month game development internship, I would prioritise a furnished student room/shared apartment. It would reduce accommodation costs and make it easier to meet other students.

Note: Unlike Maribor and Geel, I did not find sufficiently reliable current 2026 listings with confirmed long-term monthly prices in Kavala to quote a specific rent without risking giving you inaccurate information. For the report, I would therefore describe these as potential accommodation types, rather than claiming that a particular room is currently available.

---- 3. Geel, Belgium – BMI Leisure

Geel is particularly attractive for student accommodation because the city has a significant student population and accommodation is generally cheaper than larger Belgian cities. The City of Geel states that basic student rooms can start around €220/month, while studios can reach approximately €450/month.

Option 1 – Kot66 Geel

Kot66

Student housing center
0473 56 05 12

Kot66 provides 29 student rooms. The rooms are between 13–21 m² and include a bed, refrigerator, wardrobe, desk and sink. Shared kitchen and bathroom facilities are available. Current advertised prices start at €340 including costs.

Advantages:

Student-focused accommodation
Furnished
Internet/basic facilities
Costs included
Community of other students
Parking available

Disadvantages:

Shared facilities
The advertised contract is 11 months, which could be unsuitable for a 3–6 month internship
Limited rooms available
Option 2 – Studentenkamers Wens

Studentenkamers Wens

Studentenkamers Wens has 12 renovated student rooms available from September 2026. The rooms are furnished with a bed, desk, wardrobe and internet. Some rooms have their own shower and toilet, while there is also a shared modern kitchen. The accommodation is approximately 10 minutes by bicycle from Thomas More Geel.

One current listing gives prices of approximately €320–€360/month including costs, depending on the room, with a one-off €135 charge for kot tax and fire insurance.

Advantages:

Renovated in 2026
Furnished
Internet included
Some rooms have private bathroom facilities
Bicycle storage
Close to student facilities

Disadvantages:

Only 12 rooms
Limited availability
Contract length needs to be checked for a 3–6 month Erasmus placement
Option 3 – iKot Geel

iKot Geel accommodation listings

iKot is useful because it provides multiple student-room listings rather than one specific residence. Current listings include rooms around €309–€405/month, with some larger rooms and apartments costing more. For example, current listings include rooms in Holven around €309–€378 and rooms in Groenstraat/Leliestraat around €305–€405.

Advantages:

Many properties to compare
Different price ranges
Can search by location and facilities
Useful for finding a shorter-term private room

Disadvantages:

Properties are offered by different landlords
Availability changes regularly
Bills and contract conditions vary
Each property needs to be checked individually
